<p:notes xmlns:a="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/main" xmlns:r="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships" xmlns:p="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/presentationml/2006/main"><p:cSld><p:spTree><p:nvGrpSpPr><p:cNvPr id="1" name=""/><p:cNvGrpSpPr/><p:nvPr/></p:nvGrpSpPr><p:grpSpPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="0" y="0"/><a:ext cx="0" cy="0"/><a:chOff x="0" y="0"/><a:chExt cx="0" cy="0"/></a:xfrm></p:grpSpPr><p:sp><p:nvSpPr><p:cNvPr id="2" name="Slide Image Placeholder 1"/><p:cNvSpPr><a:spLocks noGrp="1" noRot="1" noChangeAspect="1"/></p:cNvSpPr><p:nvPr><p:ph type="sldImg"/></p:nvPr></p:nvSpPr><p:spPr/></p:sp><p:sp><p:nvSpPr><p:cNvPr id="3" name="Notes Placeholder 2"/><p:cNvSpPr><a:spLocks noGrp="1"/></p:cNvSpPr><p:nvPr><p:ph type="body" idx="1"/></p:nvPr></p:nvSpPr><p:spPr/><p:txBody><a:bodyPr/><a:lstStyle/><a:p><a:r><a:rPr dirty="0"/><a:t>DELIVERY</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr dirty="0"/><a:t>Suggested time: 46 seconds. Use the click builds to pace the explanation.</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr dirty="0"/></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr dirty="0"/><a:t>SPEAKER NARRATION</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr dirty="0"/><a:t>Your original explanation is the right starting point: look for patterns and use them with new data. A house-price model relates property attributes to sale prices. An image classifier relates visual patterns to labels. A generative model learns statistical structure that lets it produce a continuation, an image, or another output. These examples differ in what we ask the model to return. The common thread is learning numerical relationships from examples. Statistical patterns can be useful without proving causation, and success on past examples does not guarantee success in a changed setting. We will return to that distinction when we discuss evaluation.</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:endParaRPr dirty="0"/></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr dirty="0"/><a:t>TRANSITION</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr dirty="0"/><a:t>Next: Data comes in